<!-- CONTEXT: THE SAVE SYSTEM -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-network-wired"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">How It Works: The Save System</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="info-content">
        <p>You do <strong>not</strong> need to write any file handling code. The Engine does the heavy lifting for you.</p>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
            <div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: center;">
                <span class="badge" style="background: #28a745; color: white; padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 4px;">SAVING</span>
                <span>The Engine asks your player object: <em>"Give me a dictionary of your stats."</em> (<code style="color: #d63384;">to_save_data</code>). The Engine then writes that dictionary to a file.</span>
            </div>
            <div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: center;">
                <span class="badge" style="background: #007bff; color: white; padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 4px;">LOADING</span>
                <span>The Engine reads the file, gets the dictionary, and hands it to your class: <em>"Build a new player from this data."</em> (<code style="color: #d63384;">from_save_data</code>).</span>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Your job is just to pack the suitcase (dictionary) and unpack it. The Engine carries it to the airport (File System).</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- STRUCTURE GUIDE -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Structure Guide</h2>
    </div>
    
    <p class="section-intro">Add these two methods to Player. The engine handles the file I/O.</p>
    
    <!-- Quick reference -->
    <div style="background: #f1f3f5; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.85rem;">
        <strong>Dictionary keys the engine expects:</strong><br>
        <code style="color: #d63384;">"name"</code>, <code style="color: #d63384;">"player_type"</code>, <code style="color: #d63384;">"health"</code>, <code style="color: #d63384;">"max_health"</code>, <code style="color: #d63384;">"armour"</code>, <code style="color: #d63384;">"accuracy"</code>, <code style="color: #d63384;">"inventory"</code>
    </div>
    
    <!-- 1. to_save_data -->
    <details class="skeleton-block" open>
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">1</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">to_save_data() → dict</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                <strong>Called when:</strong> Player types <code>save</code><br>
                <strong>Does:</strong> Pack all player stats into a dictionary<br><br>
                <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.85rem;">
                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #dee2e6;">
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;"><code>"name"</code></td>
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;">← self.name</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #dee2e6;">
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;"><code>"player_type"</code></td>
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;">← "player" (or "brute"/"scout" for subclasses)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #dee2e6;">
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;"><code>"health"</code></td>
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;">← self.health</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #dee2e6;">
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;"><code>"inventory"</code></td>
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;">← self.inventory.list_items()</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding: 0.4rem;" colspan="2"><em>...and max_health, armour, accuracy</em></td>
                    </tr>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
    
    <!-- 2. from_save_data -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">2</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">from_save_data(cls, data) → Player</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                <strong>Called when:</strong> Player types <code>load</code><br>
                <strong>Does:</strong> Create a fresh player from saved dictionary<br><br>
                
                <div style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-bottom: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid #ffc107;">
                    <strong>@classmethod</strong> means it's called on the <em>class itself</em>, not an instance.<br>
                    Use <code>cls(name)</code> to create a new player (this calls __init__).
                </div>
                
                <strong>Steps:</strong>
                <ol style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                    <li>Create: <code>player = cls(data["name"])</code></li>
                    <li>Overwrite: <code>player.health = data["health"]</code> etc.</li>
                    <li>Rebuild inventory: loop through <code>data["inventory"]</code> and add each item</li>
                    <li>Return the player</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
</section>

